<section id="guide">
  <div class="guide-inner">
    <p class="eyebrow">THE WORLD GUIDE</p>
    <h2 class="guide-title">Everything you need to know. <em>Fast.</em></h2>
    <p class="guide-subtitle">Six panels. The complete picture.</p>

    <div class="tab-wrapper">
      <div class="tab-fade-right" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div role="tablist" aria-label="World Guide" class="tablist">
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-sport" id="tab-sport" data-tab="sport">THE SPORT</button>
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-tour" id="tab-tour" data-tab="tour">THE TOUR</button>
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-season" id="tab-season" data-tab="season">THE SEASON</button>
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-money" id="tab-money" data-tab="money">THE MONEY</button>
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-olympics" id="tab-olympics" data-tab="olympics">THE OLYMPICS</button>
        <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-language" id="tab-language" data-tab="language">THE LANGUAGE</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 1: THE SPORT -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-sport" aria-labelledby="tab-sport" data-panel="sport" class="panel">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">SHOW JUMPING</p>
            <p class="body-text">The most cinematic, fastest-growing, and wealthiest of the three Olympic disciplines. Horse and rider jump a course of colored fences against the clock. Knock a rail: 4 faults. The jump-off — a sudden-death speed round between tied competitors — is the most telegenic sixty seconds in equestrian sport. This is where the big money lives.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">DRESSAGE</p>
            <p class="body-text">Often called "horse ballet." Rider and horse perform a choreographed routine of movements — pirouettes, half-passes, piaffe, passage — judged on harmony and precision. The drama is slow-burning and intimate: the relationship between horse and rider is everything.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">EVENTING</p>
            <p class="body-text">Three phases: dressage, cross-country (jumping natural obstacles at speed outdoors), and show jumping. The most physically dangerous discipline — horses and riders have died on course. Think of it as the triathlon of equestrian sport.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="gov-diagram">
            <p>FEI (Fédération Équestre Internationale) — international governing body</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;└── USEF (United States Equestrian Federation) — US national body</p>
            <p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;└── USHJA (US Hunter Jumper Association) — hunter/jumper division</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <div class="dark-callout">
            <p class="callout-label">FOR THE SHOW</p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Show jumping is the most cinematic and commercially developed discipline.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Dressage provides the emotional, intimate storylines.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Eventing has the life-and-death stakes.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>The richest drama lives at the intersection of all three.</em></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 2: THE TOUR -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-tour" aria-labelledby="tab-tour" data-panel="tour" class="panel" style="display:none;opacity:0">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">FEI WORLD CUP</p>
            <p class="body-text">The premier indoor show jumping series. Runs October through April across North American and European leagues — the top qualifiers from each advance to the World Cup Final each April. The Champions League of show jumping.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">ROLEX GRAND SLAM</p>
            <p class="body-text">Four legendary Grand Prix events: Aachen, Geneva, Spruce Meadows, and the Dutch Masters. Win three in a row and collect a bonus of €1 million. It has never been achieved. This is equestrian sport's Holy Grail.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">LONGINES GLOBAL CHAMPIONS TOUR (LGCT)</p>
            <p class="body-text">The most glamorous circuit in the sport. 15+ stops at iconic locations — Monaco, Hamburg, Rome, Shanghai, Doha, Miami Beach, Prague. A parallel team competition, the Global Champions League, runs alongside it. This is where European wealth and international spectacle collide.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">WINTER EQUESTRIAN FESTIVAL — WELLINGTON, FL</p>
            <p class="body-text">Twelve consecutive weeks from January to April. Hundreds of riders base themselves in South Florida all winter. Each Saturday night features a Grand Prix — culminating in the $500,000 Rolex Grand Prix in April. Wellington is the equestrian capital of America.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="tour-table-wrap">
            <table class="tour-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>SHOW</th>
                  <th>LOCATION</th>
                  <th>WHEN</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>CHIO Aachen</td>
                  <td>Germany</td>
                  <td>June–July</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>CHI Geneva</td>
                  <td>Switzerland</td>
                  <td>December</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Spruce Meadows Masters</td>
                  <td>Canada</td>
                  <td>September</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Dutch Masters</td>
                  <td>Netherlands</td>
                  <td>March</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Royal Windsor</td>
                  <td>UK</td>
                  <td>May</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>CSIO Barcelona</td>
                  <td>Spain</td>
                  <td>September</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Hampton Classic</td>
                  <td>New York</td>
                  <td>August</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Jumping Verona</td>
                  <td>Italy</td>
                  <td>November</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <blockquote class="pull-quote">
            <p>"It moves in a circle. Wellington in winter. Aachen in summer. The Hamptons in late August. Geneva by Christmas. The same eighty or so faces — the riders, the owners, the grooms, the families — show up at each stop, set up their barns, compete for millions, and leave for the next city."</p>
            <footer class="quote-attribution">— THE CIRCUIT, SERIES BIBLE</footer>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 3: THE SEASON -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-season" aria-labelledby="tab-season" data-panel="season" class="panel" style="display:none;opacity:0">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <div class="calendar">
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">JANUARY</span>
              <span class="cal-body">Winter Equestrian Festival opens in Wellington, FL. The season begins. Thermal series starts in California.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">FEBRUARY</span>
              <span class="cal-body">WEF in full swing. European indoor circuit picks up speed. Young riders begin ramping up.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">MARCH</span>
              <span class="cal-body">WEF reaches its peak weeks. Dutch Masters (Grand Slam). World Cup qualifiers in full force. Pressure builds as Olympic team selection begins to take shape.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">APRIL</span>
              <span class="cal-body">WEF concludes with the $500,000 Rolex Grand Prix. FEI World Cup Final. Season inflection point: horses and riders begin moving to Europe.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">MAY</span>
              <span class="cal-body">European outdoor season opens. Royal Windsor. Horses cross the Atlantic. US spring shows begin.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">JUNE</span>
              <span class="cal-body">CHIO Aachen — the pinnacle. Nations Cup season in full effect. Global Champions Tour mid-season. The sport at its most prestigious.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">JULY</span>
              <span class="cal-body">Post-Aachen circuit. LGCT stops continue. Mid-season Olympic team selection begins in Olympic years. Enormous pressure.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">AUGUST</span>
              <span class="cal-body">Hampton Classic. Last chance for Olympic qualification. European Championships. High drama.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">SEPTEMBER</span>
              <span class="cal-body">Spruce Meadows Masters (Grand Slam). CSIO Barcelona. End-of-season push for rankings. Year-end points races begin to matter.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">OCTOBER</span>
              <span class="cal-body">FEI World Cup season begins (new cycle). Major indoor shows open. US National Championships. Riders reset.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">NOVEMBER</span>
              <span class="cal-body">Indoor European circuit intensifies. Jumping Verona. Equitation Finals season in the US — the junior world's Super Bowl moment.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cal-row">
              <span class="cal-month">DECEMBER</span>
              <span class="cal-body">CHI Geneva (Grand Slam). Horses rest. Riders plan next season. The year closes in Swiss luxury.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <div class="dark-callout">
            <p class="callout-label">OLYMPIC YEARS</p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>In 2028, every decision from January through June is filtered through one question:</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body callout-body--quote"><em>"Are we on the team?"</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Olympic selection is made in June or July.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Everything changes. The drama intensifies enormously.</em></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 4: THE MONEY -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-money" aria-labelledby="tab-money" data-panel="money" class="panel" style="display:none;opacity:0">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <p class="body-text body-text--opening">This is not a sport where athletes earn salaries. Understanding where the money flows reveals the real power dynamics — and the most dramatic story tensions.</p>

          <div class="price-tiers">
            <div class="price-row">
              <span class="price-tier">ENTRY-LEVEL COMPETITIVE HORSE</span>
              <span class="price-value">$50,000 – $200,000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="price-row">
              <span class="price-tier">AMATEUR-COMPETITIVE HORSE</span>
              <span class="price-value">$150,000 – $500,000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="price-row">
              <span class="price-tier">SMALL GRAND PRIX HORSE</span>
              <span class="price-value">$300,000 – $1,000,000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="price-row">
              <span class="price-tier">TOP GRAND PRIX / 5* HORSE</span>
              <span class="price-value">$1M – $5M+</span>
            </div>
            <div class="price-row">
              <span class="price-tier">OLYMPIC-CALIBER MOUNT</span>
              <span class="price-value">$5M – $15M+</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">THE HORSE DEAL</p>
            <p class="body-text">The primary engine of wealth in show jumping is horse transactions. A talented horse bought for €50,000 can be sold — after development and a winning record — for €5 million. Top trainers operate as horse dealers: they find, develop, and place horses with wealthy clients, taking a commission on both ends.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">WHO PAYS</p>
            <p class="body-text">The sport runs on wealthy clients — "owners" or "amateurs" — who pay trainers to ride, manage, buy, and sell their horses. A serious competing family might spend $300,000–$600,000 per year. The grooms who do the essential daily care of the horses earn $30,000–$50,000.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">SPONSORSHIP</p>
            <p class="body-text">Prize money has grown — $500,000 at the WEF Grand Prix, $1M+ at some LGCT events. But even this pales against what horses cost to own and campaign. Rolex, Longines, Hermès, Hublot fund the circuit infrastructure; personal sponsor deals go to a handful of riders at the very top.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <div class="dark-callout">
            <p class="callout-label">THE DRAMATIC TENSION</p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>The trainer needs the client's money. The client needs the trainer's expertise and access to horses.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>The power can flip instantly when the client decides to leave — taking their horses with them.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>This relationship is the central dramatic engine of this world.</em></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 5: THE OLYMPICS -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-olympics" aria-labelledby="tab-olympics" data-panel="olympics" class="panel" style="display:none;opacity:0">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <p class="body-text body-text--opening">The Olympics is the pinnacle of show jumping, dressage, and eventing. But the path there is longer, more political, and more expensive than in almost any other Olympic sport.</p>

          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">HOW TEAMS ARE SELECTED (US)</p>
            <p class="body-text">The USEF selects the US Olympic team through a combination of FEI world ranking points, designated selection trial shows, and selector discretion. Both horse AND rider must be cleared — a sound horse and a qualified rider. The team is typically announced in June, six to eight weeks before the Games.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">THE HORSE FACTOR</p>
            <p class="body-text">You cannot qualify without the right horse. A brilliant rider on a mediocre horse will not make the Olympics. A horse injury six weeks before the Games can end a career's worth of work in an instant.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">THE RANKING SYSTEM</p>
            <p class="body-text">The FEI maintains the Longines World Ranking for show jumpers. Points are earned at all FEI-sanctioned shows, weighted by level (1* through 5*). A strong ranking at 4* and 5* shows is essential for automatic Olympic qualification.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="subsection">
            <p class="sub-eyebrow">THE AGE FACTOR</p>
            <p class="body-text">Unlike almost any other Olympic sport, equestrian riders can compete well into their 40s and 50s — horsemanship deepens with age. The story of a 48-year-old competing against a 20-year-old for a single Olympic spot is unique to this world. McLain Ward is competing in what could be his seventh Olympic Games.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="closing-italic">The first home Olympics for American equestrians since 1984. Every decision on the circuit for two years feeds into one stadium and a single weekend in July.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <div class="dark-callout">
            <p class="callout-label">LOS ANGELES 2028</p>
            <p class="callout-body">Santa Anita Park<br>Arcadia, California</p>
            <p class="callout-body">July 2028</p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>Eventing cross-country:<br>Galway Downs, Temecula</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body"><em>The first home Games for American equestrians since Joe Fargis won individual gold in Los Angeles in 1984 — forty-four years earlier.</em></p>
            <p class="callout-body">This is the series finale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Panel 6: THE LANGUAGE -->
    <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-language" aria-labelledby="tab-language" data-panel="language" class="panel" style="display:none;opacity:0">
      <div class="panel-grid">
        <div class="panel-left">
          <dl class="glossary">
            <dt>5* / CSI5*</dt>
            <dd>The highest level of international show jumping. 5* shows have the biggest prize money and the best competitors. Wellington, Aachen, Geneva are all 5* shows.</dd>

            <dt>GRAND PRIX</dt>
            <dd>The top class at a show jumping event — the main event, usually held Saturday or Sunday evening. The word carries real weight: "won the Grand Prix" means something.</dd>

            <dt>JUMP-OFF</dt>
            <dd>Tie-breaking round in show jumping: fastest clear round wins. The most exciting sixty seconds in the sport. This is the moment that makes the highlights reel.</dd>

            <dt>CLEAR ROUND</dt>
            <dd>Completing a course with no faults. A double-clear — clean in both the first round and the jump-off — wins. Every course walk is in service of this.</dd>

            <dt>SOUND</dt>
            <dd>A horse that is physically healthy and not lame. The most important word in every owner's vocabulary. "Is the horse sound?" The question that decides careers.</dd>

            <dt>SCOPE</dt>
            <dd>A horse's natural jumping ability — its talent for clearing large fences. "That horse has incredible scope" is the highest compliment in the warmup ring.</dd>

            <dt>WARMBLOOD</dt>
            <dd>The dominant breed category in show jumping and dressage. Dutch (KWPN), Belgian (BWP), Hanoverian. Bred specifically for performance. These are the athletes.</dd>

            <dt>NATIONS CUP</dt>
            <dd>Team competition where four riders represent their country — one score dropped. The most prestigious team format below the Olympics. National pride on the line at every leg.</dd>

            <dt>WORKING STUDENT</dt>
            <dd>A rider who works — mucking stalls, grooming, exercising horses — in exchange for lessons and training. The classic entry point to a professional career. Everyone starts here.</dd>

            <dt>AMATEUR-OWNER (AO)</dt>
            <dd>A competitor who is not a professional, riding a horse they own. Often extremely successful in their professional life — a lawyer, finance, tech. In the ring, they are entirely at the mercy of their horse. The status reversal is the drama.</dd>

            <dt>GROOM</dt>
            <dd>The professional responsible for daily horse care — feeding, cleaning, travel. Does the most essential and most invisible work in the sport. Often earns $30,000–$50,000 a year while working alongside million-dollar horses.</dd>

            <dt>PINCH / COMMISSION</dt>
            <dd>The percentage a trainer takes when buying or selling a horse. Often opaque. Often a source of serious conflict. The word no one says out loud in polite company.</dd>

            <dt>ON THE FLAT</dt>
            <dd>Riding without jumping — flatwork, dressage movements, conditioning work. "She's a beautiful rider on the flat" is a significant compliment from someone who knows.</dd>

            <dt>WORLD RANKING</dt>
            <dd>The FEI Longines World Ranking, updated regularly. Critical for Olympic selection and entry into top shows. A rider's ranking is their professional identity in this world.</dd>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-right">
          <blockquote class="pull-quote">
            <p>"The horse is the only Olympic athlete that doesn't know it's competing. That, more than anything, is what makes this sport cinema."</p>
            <footer class="quote-attribution">— THE CIRCUIT, SERIES BIBLE</footer>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<script>
  const tabs = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[role="tab"]'));
  const panels = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[role="tabpanel"]'));
  const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  function showPanel(panel: HTMLElement) {
    if (reducedMotion) {
      panel.style.display = 'block';
      panel.style.opacity = '1';
    } else {
      panel.style.display = 'block';
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        panel.style.opacity = '1';
      });
    }
  }

  function hidePanel(panel: HTMLElement) {
    if (reducedMotion) {
      panel.style.display = 'none';
      panel.style.opacity = '0';
    } else {
      panel.style.opacity = '0';
      setTimeout(() => {
        panel.style.display = 'none';
      }, 200);
    }
  }

  function activateTab(tab: HTMLButtonElement) {
    tabs.forEach((t) => t.setAttribute('aria-selected', 'false'));
    tab.setAttribute('aria-selected', 'true');
    const targetPanel = document.getElementById(tab.getAttribute('aria-controls')!);
    panels.forEach((p) => {
      if (p !== targetPanel) hidePanel(p);
    });
    if (targetPanel) showPanel(targetPanel);
    tab.focus();
  }

  tabs.forEach((tab, index) => {
    tab.addEventListener('click', () => activateTab(tab));
    tab.addEventListener('keydown', (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
      if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') {
        e.preventDefault();
        activateTab(tabs[(index + 1) % tabs.length]);
      } else if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') {
        e.preventDefault();
        activateTab(tabs[(index - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length]);
      }
    });
  });

  // Initialize: first tab active, first panel visible
  tabs[0].setAttribute('aria-selected', 'true');
  panels[0].style.display = 'block';
  panels[0].style.opacity = '1';
</script>

<style>
